//
// A yield point does not disturb an overlay already written into the slot.
//
// The worry this settles: if preemption restored context by reloading program
// memory from the PDI, an overlay-mutated slot would silently revert to the
// image the xclbin was built with -- which for an overlay design is *empty* --
// while data memory survived. That produces wrong answers rather than a hang,
// which is the worst shape a bug can have.
//
// It does not work that way. Preemption in this stack is caller-driven, not
// automatic: ERT_START_NPU_PREEMPT carries a save_buffer and a restore_buffer,
// which are instruction sequences the *submitter* supplies (see
// ert_npu_preempt_data in xrt/detail/ert.h). Nothing reloads program memory
// from the PDI. A design submitted as plain ERT_START_NPU -- which is what
// these tests and mlir-aie's runtime do -- has no save or restore buffer at
// all, so nothing touches the slot.
//
// aie-rt agrees by omission: XAIE_IO_PREEMPT records only the level in the
// transaction (xaie_txn.c), and implements no save or restore itself.
//
// So the hazard is the inverse of the one I looked for. Preemption does not
// erase an overlay; but a design that ever *is* submitted with a restore buffer
// would reinstate whatever program-memory state that buffer was built to
// reinstate, and a restore buffer generated without knowledge of the reserved
// region would put back a stale slot. The thing to guard is the save/restore
// buffer, not the yield point.
//
// The probe: phase 1 does not write a payload, so it re-runs whatever the slot
// holds -- which is phase 0's overlay. If a yield had reset program memory,
// phase 1 would run something else, or nothing. Levels 1, 2 and 3 are all
// covered because they differ in how much context a save/restore buffer would
// be responsible for, and it is worth noticing if any of them ever starts
// touching program memory on its own.
//
// What this does NOT establish: that a preemption actually fired. A yield point
// only marks where the stream *may* be interrupted, and nothing available here
// reports whether one happened -- the driver counters need privileges this test
// does not have. Run under contention (25 iterations against a competing
// 123-phase workload) the results were also all correct, but the header is the
// stronger evidence, not the runs.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
// RUN: %PEANO_INSTALL_DIR/bin/clang++ --target=aie2p-none-unknown-elf -O2 -c %S/../resident.cc -o %t/resident.o
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py emit --recipe one_slot --slot-ld %t/slot.ld --phases 0,0 --out %t/d1.mlir
// RUN: cd %t && %aiecc --tmpdir=%t/p1 --get-xclbin --xclbin-name=%t/p1.xclbin --get-npu-insts --npu-insts-name=%t/p1.bin %t/d1.mlir
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py workload --tag 11 --size 0x400 --output %t/w.o --workdir %t
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py workload --tag 31661 --size 0x400 --output %t/poison.o --workdir %t
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py link --object %t/w.o --resident %t/p1 --recipe one_slot --output %t/ovl.elf
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py link --object %t/poison.o --resident %t/p1 --recipe one_slot --output %t/poison.elf
// RUN: %host_clang %S/../host.cpp -o %t/host.exe -std=c++17 -Wall %xrt_flags %host_link_flags %test_utils_flags
//
// Control first: the same design with no yield point. Phase 1 re-runs overlay
// 11 because the slot still holds it. Without this the test below could pass
// for the wrong reason.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py emit --recipe one_slot --phases 0,0 --payload %t/ovl.elf --poison %t/poison.elf --skip-write 1 --out %t/ctl.mlir
// RUN: cd %t && %aiecc --tmpdir=%t/c --get-xclbin --xclbin-name=%t/c.xclbin --get-npu-insts --npu-insts-name=%t/c.bin %t/ctl.mlir
// RUN: %t/host.exe -x %t/c.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i %t/c.bin --tags 11,11 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CTL
// CTL: phase 1: overlay 11 ok
// CTL: PASS!
//
// Level 1 -- mem tile.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py emit --recipe one_slot --phases 0,0 --payload %t/ovl.elf --poison %t/poison.elf --skip-write 1 --preempt 1:1 --out %t/l1.mlir
// RUN: cd %t && %aiecc --tmpdir=%t/a1 --get-xclbin --xclbin-name=%t/l1.xclbin --get-npu-insts --npu-insts-name=%t/l1.bin %t/l1.mlir
// RUN: %t/host.exe -x %t/l1.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i %t/l1.bin --tags 11,11 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=L1
// L1: phase 1: overlay 11 ok
//
// Level 2 -- AIE tile. The one that would have mattered if program memory were
// part of an automatic context save.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py emit --recipe one_slot --phases 0,0 --payload %t/ovl.elf --poison %t/poison.elf --skip-write 1 --preempt 1:2 --out %t/l2.mlir
// RUN: cd %t && %aiecc --tmpdir=%t/a2 --get-xclbin --xclbin-name=%t/l2.xclbin --get-npu-insts --npu-insts-name=%t/l2.bin %t/l2.mlir
// RUN: %t/host.exe -x %t/l2.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i %t/l2.bin --tags 11,11 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=L2
// L2: phase 1: overlay 11 ok
//
// Level 3 -- AIE registers.
// RUN: %python %S/../pm.py emit --recipe one_slot --phases 0,0 --payload %t/ovl.elf --poison %t/poison.elf --skip-write 1 --preempt 1:3 --out %t/l3.mlir
// RUN: cd %t && %aiecc --tmpdir=%t/a3 --get-xclbin --xclbin-name=%t/l3.xclbin --get-npu-insts --npu-insts-name=%t/l3.bin %t/l3.mlir
// RUN: %t/host.exe -x %t/l3.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i %t/l3.bin --tags 11,11 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=L3
// L3: phase 1: overlay 11 ok
// L3: PASS!
